Route B patch 0014 (updater half) — atomic validate + select + attribute.
========================================================================

Applies to the UPDATER repo, which is a separate git repo nested at
`engine/src/flutter/third_party/updater` inside the engine tree — NOT to
`vendor/updater`. `UPDATER_CONTRACT.md` records why: the updater our own Route B
cells ship diverged from the vendored pin, and this tree is the one whose bytes
run on the device.

    consumed revision before : f729f958e9be
    consumed revision after  : af6e842ccf87

ONE CONCEPTUAL CHANGE: `UpdaterState::prepare_next_boot()` — validate, then
select, then attribute, in one `with_mut_state` transition — plus
`updater::prepare_next_boot()` and the C symbol
`shorebird_prepare_next_boot_patch`. The engine half is
`0014-atomic-prepare-next-boot.patch`.

The invariant, true by construction rather than by ordering luck:

    currently_booting_patch == the patch number of the returned path

A candidate failing validation is NOT a failure of this call: it is tombstoned,
the pointer recomputed, and the fallback returned. What fails closed is being
unable to persist the tombstone or to record the boot start for the selected
patch — in which case the rejected candidate is never returned. Over the C API,
NULL means the base release, which covers both a genuine absence and an internal
error.

RETENTION IS UNCHANGED, and deliberately so. With correct attribution a
successful boot promotes the patch that actually booted, so `cleanup_older_than`
cannot reach it. No retention exception was needed; the earlier reading that
cleanup was "too eager" was wrong — `record_boot_success` was told the wrong
patch had succeeded.

MUTATION-TESTED. Restoring the old attribute-before-validate ordering fails three
of the five prepare rows while the two happy-path rows still pass. That asymmetry
is exactly why the defect survived: the two facts agreed whenever nothing was
rejected.

